Z||000009||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-23-2001||11:54 PM||||The Iron Duke may not be fast, but it does not bog at low speeds when running properly.<P>With an automatic, the 4 cylinder Fiero comes off the line very well.  After that, the Duke is pretty modest.  While I have not driven one, I suspect the standard transmission Duke Fieros are similar.<P>I thought the V6 guys were laying off the whole "duke vs. V6" argument.  So here's a bit of sarcasm for those "Dukes can only be fixed with a V6" geniuses:<P>Wow, I really had no idea the V6 was faster than the Duke.  How did you come by such insightful information?  I am impressed as I have never heard of such an idea . . . NOT!<P>Trust me, everybody already knows this.<P>Ummm, isn't a V8 the obvious superlative of a V6?  How about swapping engines in your own car and leaving the Duke guys to make their own choices?  Can you do it?  Come on now it's easy and it's CONSIDERATE.  Just answer the question that was asked and you will be well on your way.<P><BR>****Getting Back on Topic****<P>You say your friend's car bogs, that can mean an excessively rich mixture.  It could be a plugged up exhaust, old air filter, or problem with the Fuel injection.  If a sensor is bad, then the diagnostic computer  mode should report some problem (it may not be the right diagnosis, however).  Have you check for codes?<P>If there are no codes, then what about your fuel pressure?  When was the fuel pump replaced?  When was a tune up last done (air filter, plugs, wires, etc)?<P>Post more information about the car and the quality of advice will improve.  <BR>||||reg||3
Z||000010||84Bill||05-24-2001||08:38 AM||||Yeah it is really neccessary to find that O2 wire and get it plugged back in. when I have the chance I will go find the color of the wire for you to trace.||||reg||14
